#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101645} :activity/id #uuid "6fe0c524-3f3f-586e-94fc-b59fd79f1521" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048792 :topic/id #uuid "bf3eabe0-16bb-5398-8fa1-786b34f78ff2" :topic/math-academy-id 1201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102019 :tutorial/content "Suppose the function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[0,5].$ The graph of $y=f'(x),$ which consists of two line segments, is shown below. Additionally, we know that ${f(0)=2}.$ How can we find, for example, the value of $f(4)?$\n\n![](images/fe/feb899a5b4728254a153d40194220c5237eb4ef84a537e22c094cbf1639ab3c2.png)\n\nThe fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x = f(4) - f(0),\n$$\n\nand we know that $f(0)=2,$ so we can find $f(4)$ if we can figure out the value of the integral. \n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. To compute the area under the curve, let's split up the integral into two integrals which represent areas that are easier to compute:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}.\n$$\n\nThe two corresponding areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ shown in the graph below:\n\n![](images/1f/1feed4138e13b671489583ec10658b377e578d50a25a3d6be5a19d8c22d85706.png)\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value above and the fact that $f(0)=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(0) \\\\ 7 & =f(4)-2 \\\\ f(4) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "376e929b-42d8-5444-a851-fab0a8cbdcdb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 802 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e3881748-705f-5bd9-a308-539665f39a59" :step/math-academy-id 1535 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100676 :question/id #uuid "21f2e870-7d29-5175-bfb9-da53446c3a4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a852fb-e00b-55e8-bde5-d6f6e2278691" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505843}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph Without Labeled Areas"} :step/id #uuid "3c63f458-0287-55af-a609-d45520a25034" :step/math-academy-id 1536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100679 :question/id #uuid "20da9010-55f7-5e44-ba73-1c881084e4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-431"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64725151-b0e8-55f8-b910-358d4d2b1d36" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505770} {